WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.040
Next we should talk about the replication graph.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.900
This is a plugin that was introduced in 4.20

3
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:13.539
that offers a new network replication framework that deals with large numbers of players and replicated actors.

4
00:00:13.539 --> 00:00:17.089
We've seen how the default network replication system works

5
00:00:17.089 --> 00:00:21.289
meaning that each actor must determine whether they should or shouldn't update to each client.

6
00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:25.390
This approach performs quite poorly when we have a large number of players

7
00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:27.890
or indeed a large number of replicated actors

8
00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:32.509
as the server must spend valuable CPU time performing these checks.

9
00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:34.770
This is where the replication graph comes in.

10
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:39.929
It eliminates the need for individual actors to evaluate their own connection individually.

11
00:00:39.929 --> 00:00:47.700
This in turn solves a CPU bottleneck that we might otherwise get ourselves when dealing with large numbers of replicated actors.

12
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:55.100
It's also important to note that we can make use of this new system alongside the standard system of network replication that we've just discussed.

13
00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.490
The replication graph is instead built up from nodes that individually perform the work

14
00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:06.140
that eliminate actors evaluating their own connections, but we're going to cover those in more detail soon.

15
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:12.640
Lastly though, it is important to note that the replication graph system is entirely native and not exposed to blueprint.

16
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:19.670
Let's take a look at the key component of this framework that drives our functionality, that being the replication graph node.

17
00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:25.390
These nodes are responsible for building a list of actors to replicate to each client on demand.

18
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:32.859
What's also great about these is that their work can be split across multiple frames as to not take a massive amount of CPU time.

19
00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:38.959
These nodes work great with persistent objects as it will reduce the time spent gathering their lists.

20
00:01:38.959 --> 00:01:45.950
Replication graph nodes are also very much a tool. They provide a simple framework for us programmers to leverage to meet our needs.

21
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:53.150
With that in mind, we can make our nodes entirely game-agnostic or completely game-specific, the power really is in the engineer's hands.

22
00:01:53.480 --> 00:02:01.239
We can utilize gameplay to drive which nodes a given actor may sit in, allowing for dynamic control of our replication.

23
00:02:01.239 --> 00:02:08.569
Lastly, nodes are also allowed to control how and when a group of actors are updated on the client.

24
00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:14.020
So with all this information now in mind, we can take a look at the visualization of what this might actually look like.

25
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:21.680
Our replication graph will go through each node that we specify and begin to request a list of actors for each given connection from each node.

26
00:02:21.939 --> 00:02:25.939
We can see that we've got a node that will give us a list of actors that are always relevant.

27
00:02:25.939 --> 00:02:31.460
In the same theme, we can also define a list of always relevant actors for a specific team.

28
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:35.780
We can also define nodes that return us all player states of the game.

29
00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:40.780
Lastly, we can also create nodes that break up their functionality with further nodes beneath them.

30
00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:44.780
As in our example here with a spatialization node that breaks us up into two.

31
00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:50.780
One which will grab all dynamic actors in the area and one that will grab all of the static actors in the area.

32
00:02:51.039 --> 00:02:56.900
Both of these nodes will return their respective lists, which will be collated together by the parent node.

33
00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.900
So let's say we've decided to use the replication graph as our choice of networking flow.

34
00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.900
How do we go about actually injecting our own replication graph?

35
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.060
Well, Unreal provides two ways to do this.

36
00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.060
The first of which we can see here written in native code.

37
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:17.349
We can bind to the replication graph's creation delegate and return our own replication graph through a lambda.

38
00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:22.610
Another way in which we can do this is through the default engine configuration file just as shown in the second image.

39
00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:28.900
It's worth noting that if we have a game with multiple game modes that have vastly different networking requirements

40
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.900
then the native implementation will serve your needs best.
